{"authored" [{"question" "q-103792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "James and Mary have a date at the cinema theater.\nJames arrived at the agreed time, but Mary is late. After $20$ minutes of waiting, James decides to go home.\n\nJames travels north from the theater with a speed of $3.2\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Mary travels east away from the theater. When James is $0.8\\,\\text{km}$ from the theater, Mary is $1.5\\,\\text{km}$ from the theater. If the distance between them increases at a speed of $2.3\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Mary's speed?" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-103792/review.json" "file_sha256" "a7b9486c5322207a006af4839052c4e1cfd176bc0eca3b87ca5d4ba873c87d3a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-103792/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0df6ce1ffb5d29944cf65cdf75be379d8772b6a9c00ad5cbd6438510505d6fde" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-103792/verification.json" "basis" 2446 "rationale" "The literal original direction toward the theater is incompatible with the rates: x is Mary's distance, y is James's distance, z their separation; z=1.7 and xprime=(1.7*2.3-.8*3.2)/1.5=.9 km/h is positive, requiring motion away. The authorized same-session scoped solver request changed toward to away while retaining west; because the Pythagorean distance equation is independent of which side of the theater Mary occupies, that request correctly solved the magnitude .9, authentically graded option c Correct. The revealed source solution also yields positive .9 but incorrectly calls it approaching. Its original diagram draws Mary right/east of the theater with a left/toward arrow. The final study correction uses east away to agree with those retained positions and annotates the original pixels that the erroneous arrow should point right/east. All source captures, uncertainties, source grades, history and scoped request remain byte unchanged. The numerical values, five choices, correct key .9, answer fields and component identities remain unchanged. The worked closing sentence is corrected to moving away. No answer/key change requires a component or question version change."} {"question" "q-103792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/1728c9ca91ef28b19c28048f5a640d0eebbd08f504bb594bc978277fe5620d70.png)\n\nOriginal source diagram: Mary’s arrow points toward the theater. In the corrected problem, that arrow points away from the theater, to the right (east).\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Mary and the cinema theater,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between James and the cinema theater,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between James and Mary.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=1.5\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=0.8\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {1.5}^{2}+{0.8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2.89 \\\\ z & =1.7\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3.2\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=2.3\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(1.5)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(0.8)(3.2) & =2(1.7)(2.3) \\\\ 3\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =7.82-5.12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mary is moving away from the cinema theater with a speed of $0.9\\,\\text{km/h}.$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-103792/review.json" "file_sha256" "a7b9486c5322207a006af4839052c4e1cfd176bc0eca3b87ca5d4ba873c87d3a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-103792/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0df6ce1ffb5d29944cf65cdf75be379d8772b6a9c00ad5cbd6438510505d6fde" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-103792/verification.json" "basis" 2446 "rationale" "The literal original direction toward the theater is incompatible with the rates: x is Mary's distance, y is James's distance, z their separation; z=1.7 and xprime=(1.7*2.3-.8*3.2)/1.5=.9 km/h is positive, requiring motion away. The authorized same-session scoped solver request changed toward to away while retaining west; because the Pythagorean distance equation is independent of which side of the theater Mary occupies, that request correctly solved the magnitude .9, authentically graded option c Correct. The revealed source solution also yields positive .9 but incorrectly calls it approaching. Its original diagram draws Mary right/east of the theater with a left/toward arrow. The final study correction uses east away to agree with those retained positions and annotates the original pixels that the erroneous arrow should point right/east. All source captures, uncertainties, source grades, history and scoped request remain byte unchanged. The numerical values, five choices, correct key .9, answer fields and component identities remain unchanged. The worked closing sentence is corrected to moving away. No answer/key change requires a component or question version change."} {"question" "q-103792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.9\\,\\text{km/h}"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-103792/review.json" "file_sha256" "a7b9486c5322207a006af4839052c4e1cfd176bc0eca3b87ca5d4ba873c87d3a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-103792/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0df6ce1ffb5d29944cf65cdf75be379d8772b6a9c00ad5cbd6438510505d6fde" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-103792/verification.json" "basis" 2446 "rationale" "The literal original direction toward the theater is incompatible with the rates: x is Mary's distance, y is James's distance, z their separation; z=1.7 and xprime=(1.7*2.3-.8*3.2)/1.5=.9 km/h is positive, requiring motion away. The authorized same-session scoped solver request changed toward to away while retaining west; because the Pythagorean distance equation is independent of which side of the theater Mary occupies, that request correctly solved the magnitude .9, authentically graded option c Correct. The revealed source solution also yields positive .9 but incorrectly calls it approaching. Its original diagram draws Mary right/east of the theater with a left/toward arrow. The final study correction uses east away to agree with those retained positions and annotates the original pixels that the erroneous arrow should point right/east. All source captures, uncertainties, source grades, history and scoped request remain byte unchanged. The numerical values, five choices, correct key .9, answer fields and component identities remain unchanged. The worked closing sentence is corrected to moving away. No answer/key change requires a component or question version change."} {"question" "q-2589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Two cars drive toward an intersection. The cars are traveling at right angles to each other, and each car's speed is $30\\,\\text{mph}.$ If car $A$ is $12$ miles from the intersection and car $B$ is $35$ miles from the intersection, how fast is the distance between the cars decreasing? Give your answer to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/state.json" "file_sha256" "89afe13b54736576b0a57d365fdf55ba97440adc5da071877637060a6634148b" "basis" 2445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/f9396718785574a63b0e1b7f621a36fee01a4d555dc67e06cf19f17bf718ba7e.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between car B and the intersection,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between car A and the intersection, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the cars.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $y=12\\,\\text{mi}$ and $x=35\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {35}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 1\\,369 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =37\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}$ when $x=35\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=12,$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}$ when $x=35\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=12.$\n\nNote that the rates of change are both negative because the distance to the intersection is decreasing for both cars. \n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(35)(-30)+2(12)(-30) & =2(37)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -2\\,820 & =74\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-38.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the distance between the cars is decreasing at a rate of $38.1\\,\\text{mph}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/state.json" "file_sha256" "89afe13b54736576b0a57d365fdf55ba97440adc5da071877637060a6634148b" "basis" 2445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "156e1ba9a0f022ec478436db5102f8eea38de4f3485accd29c129b1d286402b2" "basis" 2445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2589" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/state.json" "file_sha256" "89afe13b54736576b0a57d365fdf55ba97440adc5da071877637060a6634148b" "basis" 2445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rocket that is launched vertically is tracked by a radar station located on\nthe ground $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the rocket at the instant when its distance from the radar station is $5\\,\\text{km}$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/state.json" "file_sha256" "89afe13b54736576b0a57d365fdf55ba97440adc5da071877637060a6634148b" "basis" 2445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/7af033f137fd76e5e3003926a598e695f4e0852b11dc27dfa84d58040144972b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch point,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the launch point and the rocket, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $y$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,000\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch point ($x$) remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)(0)+2(4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(5)(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10\\,000}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,250\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical speed of the rocket is $1\\,250\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/state.json" "file_sha256" "89afe13b54736576b0a57d365fdf55ba97440adc5da071877637060a6634148b" "basis" 2445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "156e1ba9a0f022ec478436db5102f8eea38de4f3485accd29c129b1d286402b2" "basis" 2445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/state.json" "file_sha256" "89afe13b54736576b0a57d365fdf55ba97440adc5da071877637060a6634148b" "basis" 2445 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-2941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/state.json" "file_sha256" "89afe